(Regression test covering two bugs found appending a buffer to a list:
1. The uses_strings flag was never set for this code path, so
   string.asm (where _strdup lives) never got included - a hard
   NASM assembly failure ("symbol _strdup not defined") for any
   program appending a buffer to a list.
2. Once that was fixed, _strdup's NUL-scan hit the same stale-byte
   leak as buffer-to-number casting: _buffer_clear only zeroes the
   first byte, not the whole allocation.)

a list called items is [].

(Normal buffer append, unrelated to either bug)
a buffer called "buf1" is "hello".
Append buf1 to items.
Print element 1 of items.

(Fixed-size buffer filled to exactly its capacity)
a buffer called "buf2" is 4 bytes in size.
Set byte 1 of buf2 to 'w'.
Set byte 2 of buf2 to 'o'.
Set byte 3 of buf2 to 'r'.
Set byte 4 of buf2 to 'd'.
Append buf2 to items.
Print element 2 of items.

(Clear + shorter rewrite - the exact scenario that leaked stale bytes)
Create a buffer called buf3.
Set byte 1 of buf3 to 'f'.
Set byte 2 of buf3 to 'f'.
Set byte 3 of buf3 to 'f'.
Set byte 4 of buf3 to 'f'.
Set byte 5 of buf3 to 'f'.
Set byte 6 of buf3 to 'f'.
Append buf3 to items.
Print element 3 of items.

clear buf3.
Set byte 1 of buf3 to 'a'.
Set byte 2 of buf3 to 'b'.
Append buf3 to items.
Print element 4 of items.
